      "object": "First, body satisfaction and eating disorder risk were assessed using a self-assessment method; therefore, the participants' subjective interpretation of the questions may have influenced the results. Second, the EAT-26 is a screening tool biased toward the 'thin body' ideal and may not detect the risk of some types of eating disorders. The results should therefore be interpreted with caution. Finally, the limited number of participants practicing lean non-aesthetic sports and the associated wide CI could have distorted the results."
    },
